WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.350
- Journal note to self.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.053
Dear reader, follow the North Star.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.750
Erratic remnants of starlight

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.860
sends uniform rooftops
within a five block radius.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.750
Adjusting our lens further down

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.820
into the scope of an urban city,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.330
outlines of humans mechanized
by social structures

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.170
disappear into a hole in
the ground and are erased.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.710
Calibrating the audio reveals
decompressing diesel brakes

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.230
trudging up the Upper West Side.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.403
Car engines idling even (indistinct)

00:00:39.403 --> 00:00:43.320
selling The Times across from the bodega.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.050
Location only matters
because of conditions

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.150
that create location.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.490
We discourse in dualism,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.330
operating under the assumption
conditions run parallel.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.570
That space entry relates
through distant memory,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.370
Langston and Gwendolyn (indistinct).

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.720
Hear the echo on the overground railroad,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.660
part metaphor, part homage to Harriet,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.580
who would've freed more
if they'd have known

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.350
they were in bondage.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.780
Perhaps we can be new millennium Harriets,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.820
create renegade languages,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.580
rescuing status quo from language bondage

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.270
as in them shackled
and don't even know it.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.490
Traveling through black spat night,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.860
snaking around corners into new corners

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.710
refusing a final destination.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.310
__ through the train,
synthetic glass plane.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.220
125th Street in block lettering.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.740
America appears over the left breastplate

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.873
of a man in solid blue dress,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.260
uniformity along the aisles.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.257
Everybody facing north as
if something unknown awaits.

00:01:47.257 --> 00:01:50.970
"Amazing Grace," the
homeless man in the corner

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.760
with his hand cupped for change belts.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.473
How sweet the sound no
one hears, only the drone.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.783
Because Hook doesn't exist,
2009, Harlem, New York.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.493
Answer the phone at 10:00 P.M.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.410
Offer a reserved "hello"

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:14.190
on a nebulous night filled
with pallid snow in Harlem.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.380
Respond with, "Okay."

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.020
Listen, be attentive when you learn

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.730
he died in a hail of gunfire

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.010
at the intersection of Minnesota Avenue

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.950
and East Capital Streets
in the nation's capital.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.720
After thinking, "That's f**ed up,"

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.020
thank your old college
roommate for calling.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.240
Ignore that he greeted you as Hook,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.820
the nickname you went by in the streets.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.050
Hang up.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.860
You can and can't believe
the truth simultaneously.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.980
Write D-I-S-C-O in your leather journal.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.170
Maybe this will immortalize the image.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.400
You will never forget him,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.743
but you have already forgotten Hook.

00:02:51.077 --> 00:02:53.670
Before the black birds' echo bangs

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.623
against your windowsill, wake up.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.820
Go directly to the mahogany
desk between two windows,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.880
sit in the brown swivel
chair, stare at the building

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.980
opposite your building.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.523
Rearrange papers that don't
need rearranging twice.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.803
Open your journal to the name
written last night. Disco.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.207
Remember the steel doors opening up

00:03:17.207 --> 00:03:19.410
after being closed for 18
months for three felonies

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.380
in Fairfax County Adult Detention Center.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.030
Five hours after that release,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.150
see Disco wheeling an
ATM through your basement

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:28.950
on a hand cart.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.230
Out of the wall with metal
chain and pick up truck,

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.180
he had pulled the money machine.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.830
He did that.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.010
This is your introduction.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.660
Turn on the computer.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.660
Type Theodore Blanford in the search box.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.630
Click the magnifying glass,
expect to be surprised,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.690
even though you know what
the results will bring.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.390
Don't be surprised when
you scroll to Maryland

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.753
double homicide suspect
shot, killed in D.C.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.830
One lone bird outside your
window flies backwards

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.660
at an indeterminate rate of speed

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.493
while the world moves forward.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.520
The bird is red.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.700
Look for balance in the oddity.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.390
Note that double homicide
is five syllables.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.900
Five deliberate pauses before, "Damn."

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.140
Remember you knew the
suspect, shooter, killer.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.810
Suspend court in your imagination.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.937
Add four indeterminate words
to formulate the phrase

00:04:24.937 --> 00:04:27.630
"hold court in the streets."

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:32.250
This is how he would die:
holding court in the streets.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.083
Prophetic.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.210
After reading that the now-deceased wife

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.660
had wanted a divorce, deduce
it was because of drugs.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.480
Visualize the wife and
sister just before death

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.070
in their doublewide.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.980
Try to make sense of the
blood spilled on the carpet.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.993
The red is deafening. Scream.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.310
Wait for the buzz to stop buzzing

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.803
because someone rang the wrong buzzer.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.000
There is always an echo after the buzzing.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.890
Even after it buzzes
again, don't answer it.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.150
It is not for you.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.890
Keep reading the online article,

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.070
but more specifically the
phrases forcible entry

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.140
and protective order.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.330
Acknowledge that your friend was a suspect

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.133
in his first wife's murder, too.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:19.593
A dead body in the trunk.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.550
Two days later, while
driving to school to teach,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.800
call Short Man, because it takes that long

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.903
to find someone to talk about tragedy.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.280
Tell Short Man, who is a
barber and has ten years

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:36.480
of razor tucked behind
his memory, what happened.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.680
Agree in unison that prison
will turn the brain into a hum.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.050
Agreeing again that prison taught you

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.230
to be a better criminal,
though you both digress.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.320
Both of you understand the term anomaly

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.270
but it meant that Disco was a composite

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.943
of many men who never learned to be a man.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.823
You will then ask the question
for the first time. Why?

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.790
Return back home from New Haven

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.920
before rush hour begins to bottleneck

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.750
the Cross County Parkway,

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.233
dig through the closet for the
first version of your memoir.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.590
Disco rolled the safe out
of the department store,

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.870
the first line of the paragraph read.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.240
Go to the next page where he loves to pull

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.140
the trigger of a gun more
than the torso of a woman.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.150
Flip to the page where he and his sister

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.060
distribute lead bullets
through the windshield

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.400
and the impressions of circular holes

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.790
when the discarded lead
pierces the glass of Swift.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:37.113
Pronounced. The body is a question mark.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.100
He tried to run over
the wife with his truck

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.590
and then threatened
her with a claw hammer,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.003
she told the police,

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.490
Ask yourself why this sign
didn't signify violence.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.680
What theory would Ferdinand de Saussure

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.840
classify this under?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.630
Put the manuscript back in the closet.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.450
Don't beat yourself up because
you knew he was a killer

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.163
and said nothing to nobody.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.243
Forget the double negative
your mom would correct you on,

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.863
and tell yourself it's
nature versus nurture.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.820
Justify your silence in
saying that the world

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.033
you once lived in was filled
with silence and mayhem.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.600
That is why they called you Hook.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.737
Don't block Audre Lorde's

00:07:23.737 --> 00:07:27.240
"Your Silence Will Not
Protect You" from your mind.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.583
Pretend this is penance.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:35.940
Wake up the next morning,
go back to the computer,

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.330
press any key to erase the black screen,

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.460
ignore the black birds outside your window

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.540
while telling yourself,
"This is the last time.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.160
You need to forget.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.670
But before you do, one more search."

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.600
Click inmate violent death in the news.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.510
A flood of black birds
appear suspended in animation

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.180
at the top right corner of the webpage.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.220
Ignore them, but then don't.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.660
Tell yourself this is not
karma, Edgar Allen Poe style.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.880
He did not want her to leave.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.960
She wanted him to go,
said he needed treatment.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.000
Think back to the 12-Step literature

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.910
that cautions about the 13th step,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.183
sexual fraternization with
people inside the circle.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:26.140
Feel confident in assuming that
she was a recovering addict

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.820
and understood addictive behavior.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.370
Two addicts don't make a right.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.663
Tell yourself this.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.600
Read about the interaction with police

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.463
who failed to notice the inevitable.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.477
Admit the judicial system
is failing to protect women.

00:08:43.477 --> 00:08:46.050
"I am victim" was
tattooed on her forehead,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.990
yet she remained invisible
to the patriarchs,

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.780
the ones sworn to protect and serve.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.000
Ask yourself, "Does his death matter more

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.980
than the victim's death?"

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.190
Convince yourself that
race never stops running.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.250
That memory will eat your ass alive.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:06.960
Say "I am a changed man,"
but no one will hear you.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.280
Get back in the bed, pull
the covers over your face.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.090
Remember the dream. Forget hook.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:19.353
Wake up tomorrow and feel guilty again.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:21.931
- [Robert] Thank you for that.

00:09:21.931 --> 00:09:24.681
(audience claps)

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:29.550
- And so that was a piece I
read from my first memoir.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:30.930
It's called "Hook."

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.140
And it's really, we talked
about incarceration of women,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:39.140
which is the most invisible
within what I call the inside.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:43.740
I don't necessarily subscribe
to the notion of prison,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.570
or ex-felon, or convict.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.740
I don't necessarily believe
in that s**t as language.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.240
'Cause I'm not an ex anything.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.673
I'm not a felon. I'm just me.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:55.680
You know what I mean?

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.350
So I think it's interesting sometimes.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:09:59.280
However, I do get it.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.730
When I'm in places like this,

00:10:02.008 --> 00:10:05.190
I understand the things that...

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.140
If I'm about that work,

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.100
I have to put myself on
display a little bit.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.260
So it's a negotiation.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.060
But it's my negotiation,

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:17.070
and I determine the terms of when I...

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.320
You know what I'm saying?
So it is that, too.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.583
But this is a book, it's epistolary,

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:25.557
but it's basically half me

00:10:25.557 --> 00:10:29.820
and half a woman that was
incarcerated, named L,

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.920
in Brooklyn Detention Center, the Feds,

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.367
and she was detained for three
years in the federal system,

00:10:36.367 --> 00:10:38.190
and we shared letters back and forth.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.870
So this was actually my first memoir,

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:43.410
and then a lot of the things
that I kinda did in my life

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:44.580
come to her in mail calls,

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.530
so you actually read through her eyes.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:50.847
And the main reason I did this
was because of the silence

00:10:50.847 --> 00:10:52.650
and the absence of women voices

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.000
in this whole thing that we do.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:54.833
Excuse me.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.110
Yeah, I mean, we just heard that. Right?

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:05.820
So it's not hyperbole in that, you know,

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.820
go to these spaces, the least
represented at all times.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:14.520
So I think, as I am a author,

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:18.630
but I'm not vain enough not to understand

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.400
what I'm doing with this language

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.190
is to sort of like give
people's voices too.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.850
So she's part of my memoir
with that right there.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.040
So I'm gonna read you a
little bit from "Dead Weight,"

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.040
which is my new one,

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.620
and it basically starts
off with a conversation

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:36.460
with Ralph Ellison's Invisible Man

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.350
on Riverside in New York.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.360
And basically I'm talking
to the protagonist,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.270
because I'm riffing off
of John Edgar Wideman,

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.960
who says minor characters
in someone else's melodrama.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.182
And so what I like to think
about this whole thing sometimes

00:11:53.182 --> 00:11:58.182
is how we become protagonists
in somebody else's melodrama.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.027
And so I'm thinking
about the Invisible Man

00:12:00.027 --> 00:12:03.000
and the protagonist, and
how our lives intersect

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.760
in very interesting and weird ways,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:09.123
you know what I mean, through
time, as Black men, right?

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.850
So there's that.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.070
But it's a long meditation,

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:15.480
so what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna read you

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:16.830
the beginning of it,

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.750
and then I'm gonna flip to a section

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.330
that sort of like gets at
the heart of where I grew up,

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:22.950
which is Alabama, in Birmingham,

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:27.950
a place which I really love
in all its complications.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:32.310
I love that place, 'cause
it's my home, first of all,

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:36.090
but second of all, I like it
because people keep it real.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.100
You know who you messing with.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:39.510
Up here, somebody can be so...

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:43.020
Just because the N
word, you don't hear it,

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.700
doesn't mean it doesn't
live in the imagination.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:45.807
The people that are
walking down the street

00:12:45.807 --> 00:12:47.040
__, man.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.850
And I find it very interesting
that people fail to recognize

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.173
that one single thing.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.480
Feel me?

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:59.157
Anyway, "The Protagonist in
Somebody Else's Melodrama."

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:03.630
The ephemeral foliage to my left

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:06.060
blocking the picturesque
view from Riverside

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.640
is outlined by hemp dogbane,

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:12.390
hay-scented ferns, and golden Alexanders.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.010
Before the riverbank's edge,

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:17.370
Virginia bluebells faintly
obscure the yellow water taxi

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:21.360
sputtering southbound toward
that section of lower Manhattan

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.180
resembling an upside-down middle finger

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.523
birding the Hudson on
the New York City map.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.340
100 yards out over the river,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:33.540
seagulls pipe unpredictable
trajectories in broken syntax,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:36.240
often suspending themselves mid-sentence,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.820
wading in the deaf caesura,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.350
then freefalling backwards

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:43.770
into patterns of
descriptive sign language.

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:46.620
Two BMX riders with black bikes parallel

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.840
and identical in pedal rotation

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.790
rapidly approached before scuttling

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:54.600
southbound on the walkway to a muted blur,

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:57.153
unaware of their subtext of uniformity.

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:00.450
At 139th, vintage floodlights,

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:01.890
though not lit, are pronounced,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:05.730
erect and lined single
file along the walkway.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:09.060
A mother haphazardly waves
a wand of streaming bubbles

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.190
while her trailing son and daughter

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:14.280
play a game of catch-and-burst-the-bubble,

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.550
long before the game of life
bursts their innocuous state

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.250
and reality smashes into them head on

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:21.963
with the force of a Mack truck.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.310
There is serene music in the background,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:29.700
a loud congregation of finches
invisible within the trees,

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.700
their culminating voices singing

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.683
in perfect choral structures.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:39.150
Diesel-engine buses join
the melodious overtone

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.490
with the stop and go of
their compression brakes

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:43.143
up and down Riverside.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.060
Because it is mandated by the
New York City Parks Department

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.590
to clean up after your dog,

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:52.290
there are sporadic
flashes of pooper scoopers

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.740
bending down with plastic
bags to retrieve animal waste

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:58.140
deposited on the walkway.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.740
A few more steps up the steep
incline is 145th street,

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:05.730
and the official entrance to
the Riverbank State Parkway,

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:09.120
its tall, perforated metal
sign bearing the park's name

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:10.830
in bright red letters,

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:14.040
accented by the wrought
iron gate painted green,

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.800
complete with the guard shack nearby.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:18.570
Then the view to the left bends back

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:21.270
to the brackish blue water of the Hudson

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.720
before opening onto a canopy of trees,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:26.880
as if the impending stretch of walkway

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.803
is the calm before the storm.

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:32.850
Dusk seems more than eager to descend

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.320
on this East Coast island,

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.570
and headlights will soon stab the darkness

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.573
while inching across the
bridge into New Jersey.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:43.200
The smell of yesterday's
rain is crisp to inhale

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:47.970
with each elongated
stride along the walkway.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.550
At 150th street, a small, oval-shaped park

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:55.290
has at its center a
15-foot-high, 10-foot-wide,

00:15:55.290 --> 00:16:00.290
six-inch-thick bronze
sculpture by Elizabeth Catlett

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:03.960
resting on a raised bed of grass.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.450
Carved out of the slab is
the silhouette of a man

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:09.600
who has no rib cage, or flesh, or veins.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.940
His insides are cut out.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:15.780
The silhouette holds his right
hand up, as if to say "stop,"

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:17.700
while the left is anchored to the side,

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:20.640
as if providing balance
against the turbulence

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:24.183
the world is capable of
inflicting, a sort of rudder.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.080
Next to the monument is a concrete marker

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.487
inscribed with these words:

00:16:30.487 --> 00:16:33.303
"I am an invisible man.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:37.290
I am invisible, understand,

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:40.737
simply because people refuse to see me."

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:46.083
Behold Ralph Ellison's Invisible Man.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.200
The silhouette, whom I
shall call Cutout Man

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.800
could represent me in various
stages of a complicated life,

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.790
at times unable to escape
the social pitfalls

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:58.950
that had befallen a slew of Black men,

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.690
particularly in the '80s,

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:04.020
an era that seems so damned archaic now.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.150
The residual damage caused by drugs

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:09.033
and longs prison sentences,
however, is ever present.

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:13.500
I take a seat on one of
the curved benches nearby

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.260
and study my doppelganger in amazement

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:19.770
as spectator and
spectacle, fanatical-like,

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:22.740
thinking how easily the sculpture
of this timeless character

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.720
in somebody else's melodrama

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:29.223
sequestered an unimaginable
protagonist rendered real.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.270
The unauthorized biographer of ours,

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:36.240
yes, mine and his, life trajectory

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:40.110
charted a path wrought
with racism, deception,

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:44.010
trickery, willful ambiguity, satire,

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:47.223
and comedic relief alongside
the theater of the absurd.

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.790
We were only made aware
of our line in society

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.220
through an accidental point of view.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:55.500
That moment we were
able to reverse the lens

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.300
and peer into twisted eyes

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.060
connected to a brain that
dreamed a horrific fantasy

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.463
for which there seems no immediate escape.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:06.300
Cutout Man and I fell
victim to a narrative

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:08.760
that featured us as the tragedy,

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.100
the sad sack, the bumbling idiot,

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.110
unable to control the thematic threads

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.630
within our own storyline.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.180
We could not hear the
hidden or silent dialogue

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.580
that kept our daily existence simmering

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.533
below the surface of the living.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.790
The only difference between
us is that I was born

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:28.923
not of the pen, but of the flesh.

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:31.710
Blood courses through my veins,

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:33.450
and I'm filled with bone and sinew,

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:37.713
yet my insides, too, are
invisible as a cutout.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.860
I get up from my bench and position myself

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:43.770
so that I am looking
into Cutout Man's eyes

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.393
before he can split the scene.

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:49.710
On this day we are alone in New York City,

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.960
Hamilton Heights to be exact,

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:54.930
a rarity in the city of millions.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.690
I have long held a
burning desire to dialogue

00:18:57.690 --> 00:18:59.670
with this symbol of erasure

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:03.660
about the madness that
made both of us real.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.133
This is my opportunity.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.340
Although I am talking to myself,

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:10.710
I am talking to Cutout Man.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.950
Today, he will be my therapist.

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:16.320
I can see straight through his body,

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.503
clear across the Hudson
to the Jersey shoreline.

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:24.030
He is a pattern, or a
figure traced and cut

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:28.353
from a human being, an
outline within an outline.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.363
Let me switch a little bit
to one of the sections.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:45.180
The other part is you're gonna catch me

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:48.630
talking to him in mid-conversation,

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:49.530
because it's really long,

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:51.090
but I wanted to get this one in.

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:54.930
And it deals with a situation
around James Baldwin,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:56.220
that moment when you realize

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:58.353
that we in all this together.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.460
So here we go.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:04.470
Although Brown and Board of Education

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:06.810
were decided in 1954...

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:08.460
So I'm talking to him now, right?

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.350
We're going through this whole dialogue.

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.940
Some schools and districts waited

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:15.300
until the early 1970s to desegregate.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:17.790
In Birmingham, Alabama,
oh, that magic city,

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:20.880
that glowing republic
of Southern Oligarchy,

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.250
compliance came in the waning hours

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:25.530
in the fall of '72.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:28.200
This desegregation included
integrating teachers,

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.480
which brought me, the 10-year-old,

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:33.390
to the emphatic statement
about white people.

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.760
My mother and I entered
Gardendale Elementary

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.040
as a social experiment of integration

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:39.567
mandated by the Court,

00:20:39.567 --> 00:20:42.900
and I would soon come to find
out what it meant to be black.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.360
There's a wonderful video of James Baldwin

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.030
debating William F. Buckley at Cambridge

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:50.550
on the argument that the American dream

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:54.390
has been achieved at the
expense of the American Negro.

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:56.730
The event was hosted
by the Cambridge Union,

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.970
a student debating society, in 1965.

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:01.410
four years after my birth,

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:06.273
at the segregated South
Highlands Infirmary in Southside.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:11.100
There is a moment about
18 minutes into the video,

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.990
and some of you may have seen this,

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:14.673
where Baldwin articulates:

00:21:15.997 --> 00:21:18.330
"In the case of the American Negro,

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:20.550
born in that glittering republic,

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:21.870
and in the moment you are born,

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:23.460
since you don't know any better,

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:26.970
every stick and stone
and every face is white.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:28.800
And since you have not yet seen a mirror,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:31.410
you suppose that you are, too.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:32.760
It comes as a great shock

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:34.860
around the age of five or six or seven

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.450
to discover that the flag

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:38.490
to which you have pledged allegiance,

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:39.660
along with everybody else,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.373
has not pledged allegiance to you.

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.490
It comes as a great shock to discover that

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.430
as Gary Cooper is killing off the Indians,

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.230
and you are rooting for Gary Cooper,

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:51.570
that the Indians were you."

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.200
The thing is, Cutout Man,

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:56.820
before my entry into Gardendale,

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:59.220
every time I pledged
allegiance in the classroom,

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:01.590
it had been with kids who looked like me,

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.870
kids I learned to high-five, dap up,

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.960
and shoot marbles between
shotgun houses with

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:09.573
as we meandered into our teenage years.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.810
Did you know that Tony
Morrison took the position that

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.003
something was lost in segregation?

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.810
The loss doesn't celebrate the
division the racism produced,

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.510
but what came out of that division:

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.010
a way of going against the grain

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:25.410
for the sake of self-survival,

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:28.170
taking the blackness forced upon you,

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:31.170
the nigga that you became,
and making it valuable,

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.690
something to be studied,
imitated, appropriated,

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:36.570
and commodified within
contemporary culture

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:38.703
for the sake of modern art.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:42.510
Me and the kids I once
pledged allegiance with

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:44.610
were being conditioned
to blindly pledge loyalty

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:47.220
to a system we hadn't begun to understand,

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:49.773
nor had that system
pledged allegiance to us.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:52.650
There had been dynamite blasts,

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:55.350
water hoses thrust upon the throngs,

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:58.020
billy club beatings, dog bites, hangings,

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:00.090
insurrections against humanity,

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:02.220
and violence right in our own city,

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:04.440
and yet, we remained oblivious

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:07.050
to our outer world of segregation,

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:09.270
encapsulated in our own sub-society

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.103
of a perceived otherness.

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.010
Maybe if we kids had been aware,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.470
or if our parents had been more woke,

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:19.110
we all would've revolted
against this narrative,

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:22.080
or at least questioned this
insane allegiance to a society

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.770
not yet fully committed
to our best entrance.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:30.330
And yes, I would watch the
old westerns with my father,

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:33.930
cheering for the cowboys
to do the Indians dirty.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:35.790
Even outside playing with my friends

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.670
between the shotgun houses in Smithfield,

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:42.330
I strutted a six-shooter cap
gun, full with cowboys suit,

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.400
thinking I could be the Lone Ranger,

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:47.400
when in actuality, all
I could ever hope to be

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.923
was Tonto, a sidekick.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:53.040
What Baldwin knew, and I didn't,

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:54.745
but would soon come to find out,

00:23:54.745 --> 00:23:56.460
was that there is a terrifying moment

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:58.890
within one system of reality,

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.690
a moment that would contextualize race,

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:03.750
from which there is no return.

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:07.380
True, you do become the thing to be hunted

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.330
and treated like an animal.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:11.880
The trauma is difficult to retell.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:16.880
And remember, I begin slow,
meek. Then it happened.

00:24:18.637 --> 00:24:23.130
"Say what? I can't hear
you, speak a little louder."

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:25.777
Cutout Man is pushing the
story out of my mouth.

00:24:25.777 --> 00:24:27.360
"Don't hold back now.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.190
Get it out, speak."

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.867
I come back again with,
"Then it happened."

00:24:31.867 --> 00:24:34.290
"That's it. Now we're talking."

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:35.970
I continue with authority.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:37.860
The ball was up in the air,

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.830
and Michael Hallmark, my
new best white friend,

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.370
had thrown the spiral
perfectly, and I was so fast,

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.920
I thought I would jump
high out my PF Flyers,

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:48.990
and then they were laced real tight.

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:51.180
I wanted to jump high, but
couldn't jump high enough,

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:53.070
because the ball was sailing too far

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:54.660
from my fingertips to reach it.

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:56.700
The ball hit a girl on her side.

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:59.520
I picked the ball up,
she kicked real hard,

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:02.100
called me "nigger", called me "nigger",

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.080
called me a "nigger."

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:08.070
And I punched her in her
mouth with my fist. Hard.

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:11.280
Red appeared on her face.

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.160
Red blood rolling down her white face.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:17.640
She started crying. I started laughing.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:19.200
I wanted to cry.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:22.323
She called me a "nigger",
and I hit her real hard.

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:24.723
And it felt damned good.

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:28.053
Let me be clear.

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:32.220
Eunice Pearl Davis, now a Horton,

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:33.330
but daughter to Rosalie,

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:36.810
was never a timeout or a
stand-in-the-corner type mother.

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.190
My mother never hesitated

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:40.080
to give teachers full authorization

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.030
to paddle without parental consent

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:43.503
when deemed necessary.

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:47.520
I have no memory of how I
arrived at the principal's office

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:48.840
to wait for punishment.

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.330
Maybe it was the eerie
silence surrounding the event,

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:53.580
because the teachers
knew something I did not,

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:56.580
in terms of the history
related to Black boys

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:58.740
and white women in the South.

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:02.220
The memory of what transpired
after my arrival is clear.

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:03.900
Eunice Pearls comes into the office

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.667
and says to the principal
standing over me,

00:26:06.667 --> 00:26:09.807
"Do not lay one hand on him."

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.860
Now when I say never, I mean never, ever,

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:17.133
did Eunice Pearl utter those
words in regard to punishment.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:21.540
I knew then, in this racialized moment,

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:25.800
my response to an action
was bigger than the act.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.353
But Baldwin knew that
there would come a time,

00:26:29.218 --> 00:26:30.518
and that time had arrived.

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:33.990
It would take a few orbits around the sun

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:35.970
for me to understand the enormous weight,

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:37.680
the intensity of the primal scene

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:39.180
that could have taken my life.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.290
Forget two years after
the 11-day segregation

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:45.603
from my mother, but then don't.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:51.180
how on September 15th, 1963, a
Sunday morning dynamite blast

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.670
inside a church morphed four little girls

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:55.143
into a forever memory.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.980
Forget Carol Denise McNair, but don't.

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.710
Forget Addie Mae Collins, but don't.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.410
Forget Cynthia Wesley, but don't.

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:06.960
Forget Carole Robertson, but don't.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.810
And don't forget Black boys.

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:11.670
There's always a lineage of Black boys

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.383
to draw context from too.

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:15.510
There had been Virgil Ware,

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:18.300
sitting on the handlebars
of his brother's bicycle,

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:19.860
who caught a bullet in the chest

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:21.780
from a shooter riding shotgun

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:25.410
on the back of a Confederate
flag-bearing motorbike.

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:28.800
The end result, face down in a ditch.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.110
James Robinson, angered
by the church bombing,

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:32.520
because he had already experienced

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:34.140
a moment of race realization,

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:37.710
gunned down two hours after
the blast, in an alley.

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:39.450
And make no mistake,

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.003
the police account matters
more than the victim.

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:45.660
The usual responses came after the fact.

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:50.660
He lunged, reached, looked
suspicious, made a move.

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:52.650
They always flee.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:56.490
The police fired in the
air, fired in self-defense,

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.410
because niggas be magical.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:00.630
Niggas hang themselves by themselves,

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:03.630
turn water into wine,
slip out of handcuffs,

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:05.430
be looking like every other nigga,

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:07.140
do other niggas' time in jail,

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:11.073
real and imagined, make freedom
disappear in a finger snap,

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:14.703
or turn it into a life sentence.

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:19.770
Or worse yet, niggas get shot dead,

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:22.980
and the world changes
the television channel,

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:26.463
unbothered, unfettered, and unmoved.

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:30.450
My father who left work

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:32.400
with the ghost of Emmett Till on his mind

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:34.980
when he got the call from
Gardendale Elementary

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:36.300
that I had hit a white girl

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:40.830
would years later admit he had to go.

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:42.420
Had to.

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:46.110
He had to, because I was in
a place that didn't want me,

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:48.120
that said, "Hey, nigger" every day

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:50.010
to discourage me from falling in love

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:52.410
with their idea of beauty,
their idea of truth,

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:54.300
to never mistake blackness for whiteness

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:56.040
in terms of privilege.

00:28:56.040 --> 00:28:57.810
The principal wanted integration,

00:28:57.810 --> 00:28:59.550
was part of a movement in Birmingham

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:02.040
for equal education and access,

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:04.448
perhaps was a dreamer in
believing that all men

00:29:04.448 --> 00:29:07.140
and women were created equal.

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:09.090
In his dream, he saw the need to protect

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.760
this little Black boy
from a fate all too clear

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:14.460
for Black boys who didn't stay in line.

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:16.440
The parents wanted blood, or neck,

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:18.420
and were not at all
happy their little girl,

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.940
with blood coagulating
on her face and lips,

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:22.863
got clocked in the mouth by a nigga.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:25.680
But neither was the Black boy happy

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.013
at being called a "nigger",
so we sat in a conundrum.

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:34.350
In the end, the principal
sided with his gut,

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:37.200
the question being what I had done wrong.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:39.120
What had I done wrong?

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:41.610
My parents taught me not to hate.

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:43.680
I did not call the girl trailer trash

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:46.110
or any other derogatory terms

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:48.390
I would come to know as an adult.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:50.070
Perhaps the principal had no choice

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:51.360
if this social experiment

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:53.463
was going to have the opportunity to work.

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:56.580
But what about the
below-the-surface narrative

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:58.830
by those who often control the narrative,

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:01.027
whispering in the parents' ear,

00:30:01.027 --> 00:30:04.020
"You know, he can go missing.

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:05.880
Done happened before.

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:08.580
Turn up in the Coosa River

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:10.737
with his face eaten by catfish."

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:15.930
I would never know how close
I came to being seen at sun up

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:18.240
with my neck snapped at Kelly Ingram Park,

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:21.030
hanging from a makeshift wooden cross.

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:22.650
I would go back to school the next day

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:25.380
like nothing happened,
that big ass-elephant

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:27.690
no one wanted to acknowledge in the room,

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.150
still present and accounted for.

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:32.400
The kids knew what had happened,

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:33.540
I knew what had happened,

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:37.320
yet not one of the adults
wanted to talk about it openly.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:40.620
No one cared to ask me about
the trauma of the moment.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:41.969
The fact that I hit a girl,

00:30:41.969 --> 00:30:44.550
when all my life I had been told

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:46.953
that was the one thing to never, ever do.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.190
After the accident,

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:53.430
I didn't associate with
the other kids in my class.

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:54.930
I only wanted to get as far away

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:58.050
from that racial madness
as humanly possible,

00:30:58.050 --> 00:30:59.760
because it became evident these people

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:01.413
were incapable of being human.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:04.770
I never saw the girl again.

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:06.360
Maybe her parents took her out of school,

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.160
unhappy at how white folks cared about

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:11.040
the fate of Black boys
in Jefferson County.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:12.750
One thing I'm almost certain of,

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:15.060
and would love to confirm years later:

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.910
the little white girl never
called anyone a nigger again.

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:22.200
The next year, sixth grade,

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:24.720
I was strolling to an
all-Black Catholic school,

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:26.520
planted directly in the hood,

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:29.790
across the tracks in Titusville.

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:31.410
Thank God.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:34.110
There would be bonding over
stacked heels and platforms,

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:36.900
circular Afros, and braided cornrows.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:39.480
We drowned each other in
code-switching language

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:41.610
the way we say "mane" and "gull."

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:43.470
We didn't run from our own skin.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:47.010
As a matter of fact, we
ran into it headfirst.

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:48.720
We were in a cocoon of blackness,

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.330
and whiteness had inverted to the other.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:53.490
They were the others now.

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:55.680
And we called each other "nigga."

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:56.733
But it was love,

00:31:57.570 --> 00:31:59.880
learning early in life
to take what is given

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:01.533
and turn it to something valued.

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:07.080
I had left hell and returned
to the mythological paradise,

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:09.090
if only for a moment.

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:12.480
And yet society would
not care about the scars,

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:15.150
the racial wounds bandaged up,

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.310
but would expect me to forgive those

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:20.013
who knew damned well what they were doing.

00:32:21.881 --> 00:32:23.250
All right.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:24.500
Y'all still with me?

00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:25.808
(crowd claps)

00:32:25.808 --> 00:32:27.141
All right. Okay.

00:32:28.706 --> 00:32:31.500
Make sure you're with me.
Let's see where we're at here.

00:32:32.403 --> 00:32:35.670
I think we'll stop right
here. We're gonna stop.

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:36.503
Let's do that.

00:32:38.526 --> 00:32:41.310
I'll read one poem before
I go. How about that?

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:43.260
We talked about...

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:46.230
I heard someone was talking
about the book situation,

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:49.650
so I have a poem in here

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:52.017
called "Roxbury Correctional Book Club."

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:54.437
Basically, it talks about the way

00:32:54.437 --> 00:32:56.850
we treat books on the inside.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:33:00.003
And all of these reference
some kind of book

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:07.800
that references incarceration.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:09.570
So the first one is in poetry,

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:12.660
and I'm thinking about Robert Hayden.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:15.720
The second is memoir, Chester Himes.

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:19.958
The third one is fiction,
Mitchell Jackson, "Residue Years."

00:33:19.958 --> 00:33:23.730
And the fourth one is Miguel
Pinero, "Short Eyes," right?

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:25.265
So here we go.

00:33:25.265 --> 00:33:28.830
Now this is from 289-128,
which was my D.O.C. number,

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:31.740
so I figured I would take it back

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:33.393
and claim license over that.

00:33:34.339 --> 00:33:37.237
289-128, property of the state.

00:33:37.237 --> 00:33:39.180
"Roxbury Correctional Book Club."

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:40.203
One. Poetry.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:43.560
American Journal arrives in a library cart

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:45.697
dog-eared to page 18,

00:33:45.697 --> 00:33:48.840
"The Prisoner," as if to
start here in media res,

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:52.830
Dear Hayden: The I in me no longer lives.

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:54.480
It vanished, I confessed.

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:56.310
The collective We is no longer better.

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:59.100
Yes, walls here are gray, not green,

00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:00.930
but know your poems sing.

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.650
Again, I does not exist, comrade. I do.

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:06.480
Between life and death

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:08.730
in this infinite space with no clock,

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:10.710
no markers of no time,

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:13.650
calendars to strike out the day or days,

00:34:13.650 --> 00:34:15.690
running together after lock in.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.220
I write.

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:19.890
The legal pad fills itself with regret.

00:34:19.890 --> 00:34:22.590
Ownership is mine, even in this cage.

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:25.680
A brokenness dictated by time's torture.

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:29.280
Writs, habeas corpuses, motions denied.

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:31.980
In a house lacking doorknobs to turn.

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:34.680
Yes, the cockroach caught became a pet.

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:36.960
At Christmas, my celly snorted white lines

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:39.510
to escape physical torture of the mind,

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:43.410
cascading in and on
itself, rapid then slow.

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:46.560
A program of abstinence and madness.

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:49.587
Two. "Memoir for Old Skool."

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:54.123
Gloved fist, flashlight, baton is fair.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.360
Entering the hole requires protocol,

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:00.450
ears, mouth, gums, arms, lift and reach,

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.240
bend over, spread each butt cheek.

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:06.870
Behold the steel holding cage that kills,

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:08.880
a metal mirror bolted to cinder block.

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:11.250
Old Skool stares at the refracted image,

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:13.863
age a natural antagonist of the young.

00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:16.350
Forgotten brown face,

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:19.770
a little boy once someone
knew well decades before.

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:23.970
Life before puberty, before adulthood.

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.700
Every two years, hair turns grayer.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:28.710
A nickel gone with no redemption song,

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:31.200
he will never know if change is real.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:33.930
Computer shop, religion, G.E.D.,

00:35:33.930 --> 00:35:36.690
college certificates, advocates, a degree,

00:35:36.690 --> 00:35:39.447
none changed "The Quality of Hurt."

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:42.390
Old school is in the frame struggling.

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:45.960
On good days, he reads
Chester Himes's words

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.360
corroborating street heroics.

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:51.180
How else to define anecdotes of realism,

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:53.253
or the slow fatality of imprisonment?

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:56.880
The son cannot bear witness anymore.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:00.787
Forget rope, lethal injection, or gunshot.

00:36:00.787 --> 00:36:01.677
"Fiction."

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:05.610
Half-wet, the cigarette half-lit,

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:08.070
a Bugler roll up outside walking to chow,

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.920
a fine mist, picturesque and primeval.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:13.350
Almost halfway until rain implodes

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:15.060
into baptismal for sinners,

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:19.087
so believe in uniformed
gods lining the walkway.

00:36:19.087 --> 00:36:22.020
"The Residue Years," on loan from cell two

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:24.840
came last night, the paperback version,

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:28.050
bringing characters
like Champ here, inside.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:31.890
A fool's dream, or set up by subtraction.

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:34.080
Too many pleas, illegal searches,

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:37.020
guilty at the thought
of life, though innocent

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:39.780
as the mirror shaded bureaucrats,

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:44.070
Dreaming family, I draw
wife, kid, dog in my head.

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:45.570
Time measures mathematics

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:48.210
that fail to reduce mandate to logic,

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:50.970
hidden in cells disguised as change.

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:54.690
The housing unit is an American
critique on full display,

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:57.060
cloaked in numb silence.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:59.220
There are no empty skeletons on wheels,

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:02.340
with limbs pulled apart
by a team of horses.

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:04.980
The body is now instrument,

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:09.573
and tonight, a book might
save my life after lock in.

00:37:10.507 --> 00:37:12.150
"Drama."

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:13.773
Anything can be destroyed.

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:16.860
For the skeptics, trust.

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:20.737
Donny was deflowered one
night after count time.

00:37:20.737 --> 00:37:22.800
"Clearly the structure is obsolete,"

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:24.150
says Angela Davis,

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:28.050
and I sit in darkness, expected
to write about injustice.

00:37:28.050 --> 00:37:29.850
A witness to nothing on the stand,

00:37:29.850 --> 00:37:32.040
but in op-eds, I can be quite believable

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:33.303
with the unimaginable.

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:37.830
A report from cell 23. Irony or ironical?

00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:41.400
I say truth, on what plainly
is a corrupted condition,

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:42.750
identical to the blueprint

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:46.440
overlooked within the
penal system you can't see.

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:50.340
The backdraft's flashpoint, a raging fire.

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:52.140
This suicide must be.

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:55.170
I must commit to killing the
I in order to save myself.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.453
There's no hope for the flower.

00:37:58.410 --> 00:37:59.640
Under the bolted metal door,

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:02.730
it appears sometimes one act at a time.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:04.290
Short eyes.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:06.960
The dramatic place slid from cold cement

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:08.550
from cell to cell.

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:11.400
Poet Miguel Pinero is one of a few.

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:15.030
Whoever gave us prisoners
us and our right,

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:16.830
society inverted.

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:18.600
Imagine that concept.

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:19.740
Color too.

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:22.833
This violation is only the
beginning of what you knew.

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:28.560
Come sit in the political
madness alongside the bad.

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:31.890
Perception versus the
real occurs quite quick.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:35.970
Or, of course, crimes against Donny

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.443
occurred quite frequently.

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:39.233
All right.

00:38:40.126 --> 00:38:40.975
Thank you.

00:38:40.975 --> 00:38:42.073
(crowd clapping)

00:38:42.073 --> 00:38:44.422
- Thank you.
- My pleasure.

00:38:44.422 --> 00:38:48.363
Sorry about that.

00:38:48.363 --> 00:38:50.324
- It cuts a lot of stuff off.

00:38:50.324 --> 00:38:51.895
Thank you. Thank you.

00:38:51.895 --> 00:38:54.150
I don't know how to follow that.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:55.620
Well, I guess I'll start
with an introduction.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:56.973
My name is Richard Rivera.

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:01.680
I'm the director of
academic reentry at Cornell.

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:04.110
I also do ethnographic work in Ithaca

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:05.610
among the homeless population.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:08.070
I'm formerly incarcerated.

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:11.460
I served 39 years in Ingard Prison

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:13.473
in New York State, obviously.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:20.000
But this whole event has me
thinking a lot about prisons.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:23.940
And Focault's name was...

00:39:23.940 --> 00:39:24.963
Is this working?

00:39:25.802 --> 00:39:26.635
- [Organizer 1] I don't
think it's working.

00:39:26.635 --> 00:39:28.090
- I think I turned it off.
- Yeah, it's on.

00:39:28.090 --> 00:39:29.119
This one's on.

00:39:29.119 --> 00:39:30.609
- [Organizer 2] Just go a little closer.

00:39:30.609 --> 00:39:33.085
- A little closer? Like this close?

00:39:33.085 --> 00:39:35.100
- [Organizer 1] Yeah.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:38.703
- So Focault's name was
brought up a lot today.

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:42.060
And I think rightly so.

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:44.310
He's instrumental for us

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:48.030
to understand the disciplinary
technologies and practices

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:51.390
that produce and reproduce
the subjectivities

00:39:51.390 --> 00:39:53.910
necessary for hegemonic state.

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:55.020
But when I think of prison,

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:58.380
I think it is more an
encounter with the absurd.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:02.520
So I think about Camus,
I think about Heidegger,

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:05.130
Kierkegaard, because that is what it is.

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:07.260
It's an alien place where an individual

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:08.943
is trying to find themselves,

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:11.160
places that they don't understand,

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:13.500
friendships that they don't
know how to relate to,

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:16.620
and in a lot of case, this
is what happens in prison,

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:19.410
that struggle to try to find yourself

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:21.510
and define yourself in an environment

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:23.373
that's really hostile to you, right?

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:25.429
And that's where our literature

00:40:25.429 --> 00:40:28.350
and our creativity comes from.

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:31.950
It's informed and defined
by our environment,

00:40:31.950 --> 00:40:33.420
the scarcity of the environment,

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:34.680
the violence of the environment,

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:39.270
the way the prison life is structured,

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:42.930
as a hierarchical environment,
in which the individual

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:46.620
with the greatest propensity
for violence generally rules.

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.360
He doesn't have to have
an education, money,

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:49.603
or anything, that doesn't matter in there.

00:40:49.603 --> 00:40:53.160
A sort of inverted hierarchy in prison,

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:55.920
where those individuals are.

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:57.210
But there are spaces that are created

00:40:57.210 --> 00:41:00.480
especially for individuals in prison

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:02.730
that are trying to build their lives,

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:04.980
and one of those is the classroom.

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:07.440
I don't know if individuals
that teach in prison

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:11.100
know how important the
space is for prisoners.

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:13.650
A college classroom is a place where we go

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:16.770
and we can leave the
institutional life behind,

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:18.420
the prison life behind,

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:20.760
and where we can perform as scholars

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:25.760
and engage in a way that we
feel will not be disciplined,

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:28.710
and that is a tremendously
incredible space.

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:31.020
And a lot of times,

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:33.390
in trying to articulate
our experience in prison,

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:38.390
we sort of dress it in
codes, or in scholarship,

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:42.030
in order to get the word out,

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:43.710
and I think a lot of
times that what we get

00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:48.240
is a filtered, fluffy
kind of representation

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:50.640
of what prison life is like.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.710
And I wanted to make that point clear,

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:57.710
because my own experience was like that,

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:02.220
was where I constantly struggled

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:06.360
to try to understand my place
in the world, who I was,

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:08.700
and that was detrimental.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:11.700
And I was able to navigate
my way through that

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:12.720
with the help of others

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:15.363
and some of the literature
that brothers produced.

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:20.236
There's this piece that
was produced by Yale,

00:42:20.236 --> 00:42:24.450
Doolittle, called "Zo",

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:27.600
in which this individual Ivan Killgore,

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:31.770
who's a prisoner at I think California,

00:42:31.770 --> 00:42:34.620
and he writes about
this experience, right?

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:37.800
He says, "Allow me to introduce myself."

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:39.810
He says, "Within these walls,

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:42.900
I am a socially dead person

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:47.040
whose existence has no
legitimacy whatsoever."

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:50.280
And I think this this
aptly describes the state

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:52.200
that most prisoners find themselves in,

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:56.040
and why finding ways
of expressing ourselves

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:59.760
through literature, art,
poetry is so essential

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:02.220
to who we wanna be and how
we wanna define ourselves.

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:04.080
He says "This reduction takes place

00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:05.280
in language and culture,

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:08.340
typically beneath your
conscious awareness.

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:11.460
Like people, we get our strings pulled

00:43:11.460 --> 00:43:13.860
by the forces and
circumstances they create,"

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:15.410
meaning the disciplinary state.

00:43:16.687 --> 00:43:19.380
"They have no legitimacy whatsoever,

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:24.087
which is to say no self-respect
and no sense of self-worth."

00:43:26.490 --> 00:43:30.060
He goes on to say, "Self-respect
and a sense of self-worth

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:32.943
is the innermost armament of our soul.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:38.310
It lies at the heart of our
attempts to maintain humanness.

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:42.090
To be deprived of it is to be dehumanized,

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:46.200
to be cleaved from, cast below, mankind.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:50.790
Dignity, needless to say, is
as essential to human life

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:52.950
as water, food, and oxygen.

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:54.510
Let me be clear.

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:58.587
In prison, degradation can
be as lethal as a bullet."

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:02.640
And and I have witnessed this.

00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:06.093
I have witnessed
individuals be so beat down,

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:09.003
be so dehumanized,

00:44:10.050 --> 00:44:14.147
with these microaggressions
and macroaggressions,

00:44:14.147 --> 00:44:15.950
'cause there's a lot of
beatdowns in prison, too.

00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:20.670
Or a women's prison where
hygienic products are used as ways

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:24.420
to dehumanize women,
denying them basic needs

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:25.920
or making them beg for it,

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:30.450
or manipulating it in some way
that a woman feels less than.

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:32.880
And it's the same thing with toilet paper.

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:35.610
I went to Kathy Boudin
memorial the other day,

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:40.050
and she wrote a piece,
a poem, I wish I had it,

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:42.660
about toilet paper, right?

00:44:42.660 --> 00:44:44.973
And I myself witnessed this happen.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:48.330
This guy who used to take
a lot of crap in prison,

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:51.120
take craps from the prisoners,
take craps from the COs,

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:53.310
be deprived of this, that, and the other.

00:44:53.310 --> 00:44:55.140
And one day we're quiet on the company,

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:59.280
and he yells out to the CO,
"I need some toilet paper."

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:02.280
And the CO denies him the toilet paper.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:04.290
You know, "I'm not gonna
give it to you," or whatever.

00:45:04.290 --> 00:45:06.390
And this guy just went on a rampage.

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:08.520
He broke the sink, he broke the toilet,

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:09.450
they're made out of ceramic.

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:10.620
He threw his bed around.

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:13.710
He just snapped because of that simple...

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:18.420
And denial was the last straw.

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:19.500
That's the most he could take.

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:23.724
And after that he just kind
of became like a wild person.

00:45:23.724 --> 00:45:28.170
But it's in the indignities compounded

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:31.020
that really make us question
our value and who we are.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.540
And it is only through
art, through literature,

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:35.700
through writing, through
encounters with people

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:38.130
from higher educational programs,

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:39.900
or people that treat us like human beings,

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:41.730
that create these special spaces for us,

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:43.950
that we rediscover our humanity.

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:48.360
It is this practice through
academia that I believe

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:51.090
leads brothers to
survive this environment.

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:53.640
'Cause prison is about resistance,

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:57.150
and education, and writing, and literature

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:59.160
is a form of resistance in our way.

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:00.990
It's a way for us to express ourselves,

00:46:00.990 --> 00:46:03.630
to give voices to ourselves,
even if it's fiction.

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:06.360
We always incorporate a
lot of ourselves in it.

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:07.950
Anybody who has taught in prison

00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:10.140
and has received a essay from anyone

00:46:10.140 --> 00:46:12.570
knows that a lot of
that guy's personal life

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:15.003
is involved in that paper.

00:46:15.870 --> 00:46:17.640
He talks from firsthand experience,

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:20.229
because they wanna be heard and spoken to.

00:46:20.229 --> 00:46:22.380
And I think that that is the form

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:25.140
of disturbing the status quo, right?

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:28.230
What we do in prison through
our educational programs

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:30.720
is a way of disturbing this,

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:34.980
is a way of gaining a little
power where they have none.

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:39.390
A story of mine is, I spent
a lot of time in the box.

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:40.223
I don't know if you could tell,

00:46:40.223 --> 00:46:42.660
but I used to be like a wild guy.

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:45.630
So I spent about 12 and
a half years in the box.

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:48.183
And the box is punitive segregation.

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:51.960
Imagine it like this.

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:53.730
So it's a place where...

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:57.360
A jail within a jail, I think
somebody described it earlier.

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:58.800
Was it you? The jail within a jail?

00:46:58.800 --> 00:46:59.954
Yes, yes.

00:46:59.954 --> 00:47:01.260
And it is like that.

00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:04.380
You go in there, they
take everything from you.

00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:05.880
As soon as you go in,
they put you on a wall,

00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:07.020
make you face the wall,

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:10.260
and take every piece of
clothing and hand it back.

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:12.000
Then you bend over, scoop over,

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:14.610
and do all the indignities that they want.

00:47:14.610 --> 00:47:16.020
Then they'll put you in a cell,

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:18.930
and you've got three things going on.

00:47:18.930 --> 00:47:22.890
You've got three meals a day,
you've got two showers a week.

00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:24.390
Within 72 hours they're gonna let you

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:25.860
see your property to inventory,

00:47:25.860 --> 00:47:28.170
and they're gonna give
you your legal work,

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:31.560
a pair of pants, a pair
of socks, an underwear,

00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:33.000
a pair of slippers, that's it.

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:35.193
20 pictures and five books. That's it.

00:47:36.180 --> 00:47:38.400
And these are the things that, in the box,

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:40.350
they use to control you.

00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:41.370
This is what they control.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:43.890
They control you with your
meals, they'll deny you this.

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:46.283
And my strategy when I go to the box

00:47:46.283 --> 00:47:48.840
is that I don't talk to anyone.

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:52.740
I don't talk to the CO,
I don't take the food,

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:53.730
I don't do any of that.

00:47:53.730 --> 00:47:56.970
My go-to move was going on hunger strikes.

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:59.820
So they'll put me in a cell,
and I'll just face the wall,

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:02.347
the CO's right there, and they'll come,

00:48:02.347 --> 00:48:04.563
"Mr. Rivera, you want your food?

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:08.091
The nurse wants to see you.

00:48:08.091 --> 00:48:08.940
Do you want your property?"

00:48:08.940 --> 00:48:11.220
And this will go on for 40 days.

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:12.840
I'll go on like this for 40 days.

00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:15.648
And you think, "What the hell
is this guy doing there?"

00:48:15.648 --> 00:48:20.648
And that is a way, I think,
for me to gain power.

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:22.950
And I'm gonna tell you how.

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:26.700
'Cause in the box,
everything runs repetitively,

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:27.930
like a mechanical thing.

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:29.880
The COs make the rounds when
they're gonna make the rounds,

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:31.500
they bring the food when
they're gonna bring the food,

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:33.180
the captain comes around once a week,

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:35.430
or twice a week, or something like that.

00:48:35.430 --> 00:48:37.410
And during this period
that I'm doing this,

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:39.183
all this becomes disturbed.

00:48:40.290 --> 00:48:44.250
The CO starts announcing, "Cell
16 is not taking his food,"

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:45.840
and he'll do this repetitively.

00:48:45.840 --> 00:48:47.250
The other prisoners start hearing,

00:48:47.250 --> 00:48:49.590
calling my name, and I won't answer.

00:48:49.590 --> 00:48:51.600
The captain will make
more frequent rounds.

00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:53.070
The chaplain will end up there.

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:55.140
The depo security will end up there.

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:58.380
About 20 days into this protest,

00:48:58.380 --> 00:48:59.287
one CO will come and say,

00:48:59.287 --> 00:49:02.280
"Yo, I don't know why you're
doing that, but I respect you."

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:04.500
Because I drink water and I don't eat.

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:08.280
And by the time I finish
40 days, I have to stop,

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:10.920
because they've got a standing
order to force feed you

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:12.573
in 40 days in New York State.

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:17.733
And last time I lost 68 pounds doing this.

00:49:18.810 --> 00:49:20.343
But by the time I finish,

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:26.910
they don't mess with my showers,

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:28.770
they don't mess with my food,

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:30.720
they give me the property like
they're supposed to give me,

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:32.760
they don't disrespect
prisoners next to me.

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:34.470
I sort of created a space.

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:38.040
I have disturbed the power
dynamics in that place.

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:39.660
I have gained a little power

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:41.430
where there's obviously none

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.140
in a system of total domination.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:45.600
I think James Scott wrote an essay

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:48.150
called "Weapons of the Weak."

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:49.980
He doesn't mention the hunger strike,

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:52.980
but Bobby Sands and them
brothers (indistinct) did,

00:49:52.980 --> 00:49:55.080
and that's where I got the idea from.

00:49:55.080 --> 00:49:56.880
And these are the little
forms of resistance

00:49:56.880 --> 00:49:58.830
that we could use sort of to disturb it,

00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:00.390
and writing is one of them.

00:50:00.390 --> 00:50:03.030
That was just a physical anecdote

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:06.180
about how power is disturbed.

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:08.490
But writing poetry is one of them.

00:50:08.490 --> 00:50:11.400
Speaking, asserting yourself,
giving yourself a voice,

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:14.160
or finding the medium for your voice,

00:50:14.160 --> 00:50:18.507
is a really powerful
tool to have in prison.

00:50:18.507 --> 00:50:23.400
And I think higher educational
programs in prison do this.

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.740
And then we talk about
the subject of this,

00:50:25.740 --> 00:50:27.480
which I want to bring it around,

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:29.637
the subject of this symposium,

00:50:33.390 --> 00:50:35.040
imagination and incarceration.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:36.690
When I heard that, like you,

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:39.120
I was struck by the singular of mind.

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:39.953
Was it you?

00:50:39.953 --> 00:50:41.813
Somebody else mentioned
the singular of mind.

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:46.320
And I thought about how come
no one is speaking about

00:50:46.320 --> 00:50:49.710
what informs the prisoner's
imagination, right?

00:50:49.710 --> 00:50:51.240
And I think it is important to understand

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:52.710
that the prisoner's imagination

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:56.190
is informed by their
environment, of course,

00:50:56.190 --> 00:50:58.410
by the cultural norms that
are developed in there,

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:02.490
by the economy, by the systems
of what I call prison life.

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:03.840
The environment in the yard,

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:06.480
where prisoners are making things happen

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:10.770
in an environment that's defined
by scarcity and violence.

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:13.560
So they'll have nine weight courts

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:16.503
and 1,100 prisoners that wanna work out.

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:19.440
And prisoners will fight for those courts.

00:51:20.820 --> 00:51:24.210
They'll have at services only 40 people

00:51:24.210 --> 00:51:25.950
allowed in the room at services.

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:27.480
So the other Christians or Muslims

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:31.140
will have to wait somewhere else.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:33.870
Only 10% of the population is prison rich.

00:51:33.870 --> 00:51:36.420
That means that they get
regular packages and food

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:38.040
and support from family,

00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:40.380
and they sort of become
victims in a lot of sense,

00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:42.720
or have to participate in
the black market in some way,

00:51:42.720 --> 00:51:45.123
because this is the way the system is.

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:49.650
But this informs us.

00:51:49.650 --> 00:51:51.840
So again, in the box, right?

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:56.670
In the box, we have 43
cells on the company.

00:51:56.670 --> 00:51:58.800
They've got solid doors,

00:51:58.800 --> 00:52:01.920
and I have a cigarette in one cell,

00:52:01.920 --> 00:52:05.670
and this guy will have matches in 23 cell.

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:08.130
There's no porters out,
there's nothing happening.

00:52:08.130 --> 00:52:08.963
So what do we do?

00:52:08.963 --> 00:52:13.590
We take the toothpaste,
and we'll break it in half,

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:15.540
put wet toilet paper in there,

00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:18.510
take our underwears, take the strings out,

00:52:18.510 --> 00:52:21.150
tie it to the thing, and slide
it under the door real fast,

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:23.970
and it'll travel like 15, 20 cells.

00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:26.460
And the guy will do the
same thing over there.

00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:29.040
Or fixing a hot pot with a nail clipper.

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:31.350
I mean, the ingenuity and creativeness

00:52:31.350 --> 00:52:33.990
that this environment produces

00:52:33.990 --> 00:52:36.990
I think works its way into the way

00:52:36.990 --> 00:52:40.500
we express ourselves creatively,
expresses in our writing,

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:42.330
in our codes that we switch.

00:52:42.330 --> 00:52:43.950
So when a writer writes from prison,

00:52:43.950 --> 00:52:46.590
he's actually speaking to two audiences.

00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:49.590
He's speaking to you in a
way that you will understand,

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:53.280
but I know what he means
when he uses coded language.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:55.080
There's a difference there.

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:58.110
And that got me thinking because of this,

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.390
but it also got me thinking because

00:53:00.390 --> 00:53:02.100
the further I've moved away from prison,

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:04.410
the more I've been thinking about it.

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:08.640
And to end, I wanna read
a piece from Dwayne Betts.

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:11.490
I wanna honor the Dwayne
Betts. He's my boy.

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:13.380
And he writes a piece
here called "Confession,"

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:18.180
which I think speaks to that
getting closer to the prison

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:21.810
as you move further away,
and I think it's important.

00:53:21.810 --> 00:53:23.610
All right, so it goes...

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:25.290
I'm gonna mess it up, 'cause
I know I'ma mess it up,

00:53:25.290 --> 00:53:27.166
so you don't report it back to him.

00:53:27.166 --> 00:53:28.260
- [Randall] No, I'ma tell him.

00:53:28.260 --> 00:53:29.093
I'm gonna text him right now.
- You wanna read it for me?

00:53:29.093 --> 00:53:30.261
- No, I'm just playing.

00:53:30.261 --> 00:53:32.040
(Randall laughs)

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:33.347
I got him on speed dial.

00:53:33.347 --> 00:53:34.696
(Randall laughs)

00:53:34.696 --> 00:53:36.896
- If I told her how often
that I thought of prison,

00:53:36.896 --> 00:53:39.218
she would walk out.

00:53:39.218 --> 00:53:41.400
She would walk out the door that leads

00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:44.700
just as much to madness
as any home would desire.

00:53:44.700 --> 00:53:47.070
She would walk out and never return.

00:53:47.070 --> 00:53:50.430
My employers will call
me a liar and fire me.

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:53.730
My dreams are not all nightmares,

00:53:53.730 --> 00:53:56.760
but this history has
turned my mind's landscape

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:59.640
into a dragoon.

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.770
I do not sing.

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:04.500
Have lied for so many months now

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:06.990
that truth Harbingers lost.

00:54:06.990 --> 00:54:11.970
Sleeping beside her when a
memory is holding me tight,

00:54:11.970 --> 00:54:15.420
as she did before the lies
turned everything into a battle.

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:17.700
I once gasped, and launched,

00:54:17.700 --> 00:54:19.590
and tried to strangle the pillow

00:54:19.590 --> 00:54:21.450
which she placed beneath my head.

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:23.640
Imagine me explaining that to her,

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:26.553
while still shivering like
a panicked and broken man.

00:54:28.519 --> 00:54:31.290
I stopped believing in
God long before then.

00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:32.520
But that night,

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:34.950
when outside there was
no light, but darkness,

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:39.950
I saw something of what
inevitable has touched me.

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:43.320
My children slept with their lights on.

00:54:43.320 --> 00:54:45.600
I walked into their still lit room.

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:50.490
My son was asleep, and his
brother draped over his body

00:54:50.490 --> 00:54:52.110
as if he were a pillow.

00:54:52.110 --> 00:54:53.370
The way he loved his brother

00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:57.030
was everything my time
in the cell denied me.

00:54:57.030 --> 00:55:00.760
If I told my woman that
she would want to know

00:55:01.830 --> 00:55:04.470
if I thought I deserve all that loss.

00:55:04.470 --> 00:55:06.780
Her mother wonders why I won't let it go

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.843
and holds onto the happiness
and the life we have.

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:14.850
But how do I explain that
outside on nights like this

00:55:14.850 --> 00:55:19.200
is where I first learned
just how violent I might be,

00:55:19.200 --> 00:55:21.720
that I think of prison
because in all these years,

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:24.723
I still cannot pronounce
the name of my victim.

00:55:27.219 --> 00:55:28.950
- [Randall] You done good, dog.

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:29.783
You done good.

00:55:30.840 --> 00:55:31.835
I'ma give you kudos.

00:55:31.835 --> 00:55:33.601
Five stars.
- Thank you.

00:55:33.601 --> 00:55:35.969
(audience claps)

00:55:35.969 --> 00:55:37.033
- [Randall] I'm definitely
gonna let him know.

00:55:37.033 --> 00:55:37.866
(audience claps)

00:55:37.866 --> 00:55:40.283
- If there's any questions...

00:55:41.355 --> 00:55:43.938
(gentle music)

